363
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:47,820
All right.

364
00:13:48,180 --> 00:13:51,300
So remember we started with expected value maximization

365
00:13:51,660 --> 00:13:53,340
and expected utility maximization

366
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:54,800
as an improved theory.

367
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:59,920
So you could imagine a theory of economic action under uncertainty

368
00:14:00,460 --> 00:14:03,680
where all that everybody does is maximize the expected value

369
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:04,540
of whatever they do.

370
00:14:05,280 --> 00:14:08,580
So in that case, if you have a one in a million chance

371
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:12,520
of a trillion dollars, or you could have $100,000 for sure,

372
00:14:12,860 --> 00:14:14,840
then everyone would go for the one in a million chance

373
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:16,820
of a trillion dollars because that is a higher expected value.

374
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:18,440
All right.

375
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:22,040
So. And if your intuition is, yeah, I would totally go

376
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:23,700
for that one in a million chance of a trillion dollars,

377
00:14:23,700 --> 00:14:25,440
then this might make sense to you.

378
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:28,060
But most people don't think that way.

379
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:31,080
And most people don't think that most people think that way.

380
00:14:31,080 --> 00:14:34,720
So that's where we bring in expected utility maximization

381
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:39,060
as an improved theory that can explain people's reluctance

382
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:42,600
to go and take these extreme long shots

383
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:43,800
with high expected value.

384
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:43,900
All right.

385
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:47,760
So anyway, those are the cases of risk.

386
00:14:48,260 --> 00:14:51,700
Now suppose that you choose between X dollars

387
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:57,020
with a probability of one, or you're going to have X over Q

388
00:14:57,020 --> 00:15:00,000
dollars with a probability of P equal to Q.

389
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,120
All right.

390
00:15:01,940 --> 00:15:06,300
Now a simple taxonomy that economists use is

391
00:15:06,300 --> 00:15:09,020
if you're indifferent between the sure thing

392
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:10,640
and the gamble, then you are risk neutral.

393
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:12,500
All right.

394
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:15,760
If you prefer the sure thing to gamble, you are risk averse.

395
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:18,060
And if you prefer the gamble to the sure thing,

396
00:15:18,060 --> 00:15:18,820
you are risk preferring.

397
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:22,240
Non-economists often say risk averse

398
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:24,580
when what they mean is low expected value.

399
00:15:26,440 --> 00:15:28,460
So and I've even heard people say, well, of course,

400
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:28,980
I'm risk averse.

401
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:30,900
Who wouldn't be risk averse because I'd rather not have the

402
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:31,760
risk than have the risk.

403
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:33,940
That's not what risk aversion means.

404
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:37,300
So risk aversion does not mean that you would rather get rid

405
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:38,520
of a risk at no cost.

406
00:15:39,080 --> 00:15:40,980
Rather, it means that if you have a choice

407
00:15:40,980 --> 00:15:44,440
between two gambles and one is,

408
00:15:44,440 --> 00:15:46,140
and they both have the same expected value,

409
00:15:46,140 --> 00:15:48,920
but one is certain and one is not, risk aversion means

410
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:52,420
that you strictly prefer the one that is certain to the one

411
00:15:52,420 --> 00:15:53,440
that is not.

412
00:15:53,440 --> 00:15:55,480
So the expected values are the same,

413
00:15:55,800 --> 00:15:58,060
but of course the actual distributions are different,

414
00:15:58,060 --> 00:16:01,200
right, because the certainty has certainty of one outcome

415
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:03,100
and the one where it's not certain means there's a range

416
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:03,720
of possibilities.

417
00:16:04,980 --> 00:16:06,180
Okay. All right.

418
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:10,040
Now most economic models assume that actors are risk averse.

419
00:16:10,880 --> 00:16:13,000
It's a standard to assume that actors are risk averse.

420
00:16:13,900 --> 00:16:14,880
So actors.

421
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:16,640
Although it's also very common

422
00:16:16,640 --> 00:16:18,940
to assume that firms are risk neutral.

423
00:16:19,620 --> 00:16:24,340
It is abnormal to ever assume that anyone's risk preferring,

424
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:25,160
although you can do it.

425
00:16:25,160 --> 00:16:28,580
But normally we think of individual people

426
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:30,880
as being risk averse and firms as being risk neutral.

427
00:16:31,440 --> 00:16:33,280
Why would firms be risk neutral?

428
00:16:33,620 --> 00:16:36,520
Common story is well, since the shareholders can diversify,

429
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:40,240
no one needs to actually have a lot of stock in any one company,

